      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We treated 12 shoulders in ten patients with irreparable rotator-cuff tears by transfer of the latissimus dorsi. There were nine men and one woman. Their average age was 64.0 years and the average follow-up was 35.6 months (26 to 42). The results were excellent in four shoulders, good in four, fair in one, and poor in three. Active forward flexion improved from a preoperative average of 99 degrees to a postoperative average of 135 degrees. Osteoarthritic changes appeared in five shoulders and proximal migration of the humeral head progressed in six. EMG revealed that nine of the 12 transferred muscles showed activity which was synergistic with the supraspinatus on external rotation with abduction. We conclude that latissimus dorsi transfer can be effective in restoring shoulder function after massive irreparable tears of the rotator cuff."},
